  "narrative_findings": [
    "The threshold sensitivity sweep shows that lower percentiles (70th\u201385th) maximise recall but increase FPR significantly; the optimal F1 is achieved at the 80th percentile (F1 = 0.9203), which differs from the current 95th-percentile operating point.",
    "Zero-day recall is highest for DoS and Probe attacks, which generate reconstruction errors well above the normal-traffic threshold even without appearing in threshold calibration; R2L and U2R achieve substantially lower zero-day detection rates.",
    "R2L attacks (e.g., password guessing, FTP write, HTTP tunnel) share many statistical features with legitimate user sessions \u2014 low byte counts, standard ports, normal flags \u2014 so the autoencoder reconstructs them with low error, making them the hardest class to detect in a zero-day scenario.",
    "The 95th-percentile threshold provides a reasonable balance (~5 % FPR on normal traffic), but tuning to the 80th percentile maximises F1; for deployments where missing R2L attacks is unacceptable, a lower threshold or a hybrid supervised/unsupervised approach is recommended."
  ]
